Hi Perry,

Additionally, I did some experiments to know more about your previous comment about slow mixing after adding predictive nodes:

"It still may not be a good idea to include stochastic posterior predictive nodes, because they will slow your mixing."

When I changed the likelihood function in the above code into Gaussian (instead of student-t), the mixing was really slow and I started doubting about convergence. So I agree with your comment.

However, I tried to think about how to write a separate NIMBLE model for posterior prediction but it was not successful - I couldn't find an example from the NIMBLE Manual or anywhere else. In R, I can easily generate multiple samples with multiple mean values (i.e., a vector of mean values from posterior samples) and sd's, but as far as I know, the predictive node (i.e., zy_p in the code below) can be indexed using only one dimension, i.e., zy_p[i], not zy_p[I, number_of_samples].  Maybe, I don't fully understand this. In Stan, there is a block called "generated quantities", which makes it easy to generate posterior predictions. I guess this can be done in nimbleCode or nimbleFunction more explicitly, but I don't see an immediate solution due to lack of knowledge.

The following is a code block (part of my code above) inside my nimbleCode from which I want to generate posterior predictive values (i.e., zy_p) given the new predictors zx_pred and posterior parameter zbeta0,  zbeta, zsigma and df_n.

Any advice or practical comments would be appreciated.

Thanks very much.

- Seongeun

for ( i in 1:N_pred ) {

zy_p[i] ~ dt( zbeta0 + sum( zbeta[1:Nx] * zx_pred[i,1:Nx] ), sigma = z_sigma , df=df_n)

y_pred[i] <- zy_p[i] * y_sd + y_m

}

}

